<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Deister</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Deister is a wooded ridge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-saxony">Lower Saxony</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a>, rising to about 405 metres. Running north-west to south-east between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weser">Weser</a> hills and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leine">Leine</a> valley, it formed the western edge of the medieval territory of Calenberg. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">Coal</a> was mined there from the 17th to the 20th century, and today it is a popular recreation area for the Hanover region.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/deister.jpg" alt="Deister" width="320" height="269" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Langläufer 12:03, 29. Okt. 2006 (CET) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Deister.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
